\"kind\\\": \\\"Table\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Multiple matching references retained; scope needs review\\\"}}, {\\\"file\\\": \\\"data/requirements/TRAFICOM_full.txt\\\", \\\"reference\\\": {\\\"key\\\": \\\"6\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"Table\\\", \\\"reason\\\": \\\"Multiple matching references retained; scope needs review\\\"}}, {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"source\\\", \\\"reference\\\": \\\"UR P0\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"MISSING_DOCUMENT\\\"}]\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"unresolvedSourceContext\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E465\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#passengerShip> <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#sourceRequirementId> \\\"IMO-070\\\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E466\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#ship> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> \\\"Ship\\\"@en .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E467\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#oilTanker> <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#roleDecisionBasis> \\\"Explicit engineering entity/artifact class set\\\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E468\", \"kind\": \"SOURCE\", \"text\": \"                   Table 6-16: Load cases for azimuthing thruster ice impact loads.\\n                     Force      Loaded area\\nLoad case T1a        Fti        Uniform distributed load or\\nSymmetric longi-                uniform pressure, which are\\ntudinal ice impact              applied symmetrically on the\\non thruster                     impact area.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nLoad case T1b        50% of    Uniform distributed load or\\nNon-symmetric        Fti       uniform pressure, which are\\nlongitudinal ice               applied on the other half of\\nimpact on thruster             the impact area.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nLoad case T1c        Fti       Uniform distributed load or\\nNon-symmetric                  uniform pressure, which are\\nlongitudinal ice               applied on the impact area.\\nimpact on nozzle               Contact area is equal to the\\n                               nozzle thickness (Hnz)*the\\n                               contact height (Hiced).\\n\\n\\nLoad case T2a        Fti       Uniform distributed load or\\nSymmetric longi-               uniform pressure, which are\\ntudinal ice impact             applied symmetrically on the\\non propeller hub               impact area.\\n\\n\\n\\nLoad case T2b        50% of    Uniform distributed load or\\nNon-symmetric        Fti       uniform pressure, which are\\nlongitudinal ice               applied on the other half of\\nimpact on propel-              the impact area.\\nler hub\\n\\n\\nLoad case T3a        Fti       Uniform distributed load or\\nSymmetric lateral              uniform pressure, which are\\nice impact on                  applied symmetrically on the\\nthruster body                  impact area.\\n\\f                                                                                                Regulation\\n                                                                                                      55 (65)\\n                                                                             TRAFICOM/68863/03.04.01.00/2021\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nLoad case T3b         Fti        Uniform distributed load or\\nNon-symmetric                    uniform pressure, which are\\nlateral ice impact               applied on the impact area.\\non thruster body                 Nozzle contact radius R to be\\nor nozzle                        taken from the nozzle length\\n                                 (Lnz).\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  The ice impact contact load must be calculated using formula 6.46. The related parameter val-\\n  ues are given in Table 6-17. The design operation speed in ice can be derived from Tables 6-\\n  18 and 6-19, or the ship in question’s actual design operation speed in ice can be used. The\\n  longitudinal impact speed in Tables 6-18 and 6-19 refers to the impact in the thruster’s main\\n  operational direction. For the pulling propeller configuration, the longitudinal impact speed is\\n  used for load case T2, impact on hub; and for the pushing propeller unit, the longitudinal im-\\n  pact speed is used for load case T1, impact on thruster end cap. For the opposite direction, the\\n  impact speed for transversal impact is applied.\\n\\n\\n                              𝐹𝐹𝑡𝑡𝑡𝑡 = 𝐶𝐶𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷 34.5 𝑅𝑅𝑐𝑐0.5 (𝑚𝑚𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖𝑖 𝑣𝑣𝑠𝑠2 )0.333 [kN],            (6.46)\\n\\n\\n  where\\n                Rc is the impacting part sphere radius, see Figure 6-9 [m]\\n                mice is the ice block mass [kg]\\n                vs is the ship speed at the time of contact [m/s]\\n                𝐶𝐶𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷𝐷 is the dynamic magnification factor for impact loads.\\n\\n  CDMI shall be taken from Table 6-17 if unknown.\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  Figure 6-9. Dimensions used for Rc\\n\\f                                                                                      Regulation\\n                                                                                             56 (65)\\n                                                               TRAFICOM/68863/03.04.01.00/2021\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nFor impacts on non-hemispherical areas, such as the impact on the nozzle, the equivalent im-\\npact sphere radius must be estimated using the equation below.\\n\\n                                                   𝐴𝐴                                       (6.47)\\n                                        𝑅𝑅𝑐𝑐𝑐𝑐𝑐𝑐 = �𝜋𝜋 [m]\\n\\n\\nIf the 2*Rceq is greater than the ice block design thickness Hiced, Rceq is set to half of the ice\\nblock design thickness. This limitation is not valid for impact on the propeller hub or thruster\\nend cap (load cases T1a and T2a). For the impact on the thruster side, the pod body diameter\\ncan be used as a basis for determining the radius Rc. For the impact on the propeller hub, the\\nhub diameter can be used as a basis for the radius Rc.\\n\\n          Table 6-17: Parameter values for ice dimensions and dynamic magnification\\n                                         IA Super      IA         IB         IC\\n     Thickness of the design ice block     1.17 m            1.0 m     0.8m       0.67 m\\n     impacting thruster (Hiced)\\n     Extreme ice block mass (mice)         8670kg            5460 kg   2800 kg    1600 kg\\n\\n\\n     CDMI (if not known)                   1.3               1.2       1.1        1\\n\\f                                                                                        Regulation\\n                                                                                             57 (65)\\n                                                             TRAFICOM/68863/03.04.01.00/2021\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                      Table 6-18: Impact speeds for aft centerline thruster\\n                                         IA Super       IA           IB            IC\\n     Longitudinal impact in main     op-   6 m/s          5 m/s        5 m/s       5 m/s\\n     erational direction\\n     Longitudinal impact in reversing      4 m/s          3 m/s        3 m/s       3 m/s\\n     direction (pushing unit propeller\\n     hub or pulling unit cover end cap\\n     impact)\\n     Transversal impact in bow first op-   3 m/s          2 m/s        2 m/s       2 m/s\\n     eration\\n     Transversal impact in stern first     4 m/s          3 m/s        3 m/s       3 m/s\\n     operation (double acting ship)\\n\\n\\n\\n          Table 6-19: Impact speeds for aft wing, bow centerline and bow wing thrusters\\n                                           IA Super     IA           IB        IB\\n     Longitudinal impact in main     op-   6 m/s          5 m/s        5 m/s       5 m/s\\n     erational direction\\n     Longitudinal impact in reversing      4 m/s          3 m/s        3 m/s       3 m/s\\n     direction (pushing unit propeller\\n     hub or pulling unit cover end cap\\n     impact)\\n     Transversal impact                    4 m/s          3 m/s        3 m/s       3 m/s\\n\\n\\n\", \"origin\": {\"file\": \"data/requirements/TRAFICOM_full.txt\", \"authority\": \"User-supplied retained source document; interpretations are not independently verified\", \"selection\": {\"start\": 146319, \"end\": 152887}}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E469\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#iceClassIaSuper> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> \\\"IA Super\\\"@en .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E470\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#oilTanker> <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#sourceRequirementId> \\\"IMO-109\\\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E471\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#iceClassIb> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> \\\"IB\\\"@en .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E472\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#passengerShip> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> \\\"Passenger ship\\\"@en .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E473\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#markingPlainlyVisibleInIceConditions> <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#sourceRequirementId> \\\"TRF-133\\\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .\", \"origin\": {\"context_field\": \"ontology_evidence\"}}, {\"evidence_id\": \"E474\", \"kind\": \"RDF_FACT\", \"text\": \"<https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#iceClassRegulationEdition2002> <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#draftConceptId> \\\"VOC-DEV-B01-114\\\"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> 